    {
    "nodeID" : "01a0",
    "exitNodes":["01b0"],
    "heading": "Introduction",
    "content": 
    {
        "slide01":
        [
            "Ulcerative colitis (UC) is an inflammatory bowel disease that affects the colon (large intestine), causing inflammation starting in the rectum moving up through the large intestine (sigmoid, descending, transverse, ascending).",
            "There are various medications and surgical options available to people with ulcerative colitis. This module will discuss how the surgical options relate to fertility and pregnancy. "
        ],
        "slide02":
        [
            "Many people with ulcerative colitis (UC) are treated with medications, but approximately 1 in 10 (10%)  will require surgery within 10 years of diagnosis.",
            "In some cases, ulcerative colitis (UC) surgery is needed urgently due to a medical emergency. "
        ]
    },
    "media":
    {
        "slide01":"",
        "slide02":""
    }
    },
    {
    "nodeID" : "01b0",
    "exitNodes":["01c1","03a0"],
    "heading": "UC Disease and Symptoms",
    "content": 
    {
        "slide01":
        [
            "The most important thing for ulcerative colitis (UC) patients who are considering or trying to get pregnant, is to understand that for fertility and pregnancy outcomes, active disease is the most important factor. ",
            "Active disease in the preconception time reduces fertility, and increases risk of active disease during pregnancy.",
            "Active disease during pregnancy increases the risk of adverse outcomes such as pregnancy loss, premature birth, smaller-than-expected babies, and babies that grow more slowly than expected. This will then impact the baby's long term health. ",
            "Thus it is recommended to achieve disease remission before pregnancy whether by medical treatments or surgical treatments."
        ],
        "slide02":
        [
            "Ulcerative colitis (UC) causes inflammation and ulcers in your colon (large intestine).",
            "Symptoms of active ulcerative colitis can include: ",
            "abdominal cramps, ",
            "bloody stools, ",
            "frequent stools, ",
            "and urgency to pass stool."
        ],
        "slide03":
        [
            "Ulcerative colitis (UC) patients are often treated with medication to control the inflammation and relieve their symptoms, but when these don’t work for the patient, surgical treatment of the digestive tract may be recommended."
        ]
    },
    "media":
    {
        "slide01":"",
        "slide02":""
    }
    },
